WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.768
Secure hashing often involves large files, network streams, backups, update packages, or logs.

2
00:00:04.768 --> 00:00:08.802
Loading all data into memory may be unnecessary, inefficient, or unsafe.

3
00:00:08.802 --> 00:00:13.937
Incremental hashing lets a program process data in chunks while updating a hash context.

4
00:00:13.937 --> 00:00:19.438
This is a good fit for C++ when resource ownership and error handling are explicit.

5
00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:20.538
Every read matters.

6
00:00:20.538 --> 00:00:26.407
A partial read, truncated file, stream error, file change, or failed close can invalidate the result.

7
00:00:26.407 --> 00:00:30.441
Verification code should not silently accept a digest from incomplete data.

8
00:00:30.441 --> 00:00:34.108
File metadata is also not the same as file content.

9
00:00:34.108 --> 00:00:43.277
If size, name, permissions, timestamp, or other metadata is part of the security decision, the design must say so and include it in a documented

10
00:00:43.277 --> 00:00:43.644
representation.

11
00:00:43.644 --> 00:00:47.312
RAII should manage file handles, buffers, and hashing context cleanup.

12
00:00:47.312 --> 00:00:55.747
The code should define what happens when input changes during hashing, when the expected digest has the wrong format, when the algorithm is

13
00:00:55.747 --> 00:00:57.948
unsupported, or when a read fails.

14
00:00:57.948 --> 00:01:04.549
Hash verification should fail safely and report enough diagnostic context to fix the problem without exposing sensitive data.

15
00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:10.418
Large-data hashing is not just a loop; it is an integrity workflow with error paths.

16
00:01:10.418 --> 00:01:18.486
Teams should test interruption, short reads, renamed files, and storage failures because those are the cases most likely to separate a robust

17
00:01:18.486 --> 00:01:20.320
verifier from a fragile one.
